# draft_defense_id|display_name|definition|mechanism|applicable_conditions|limitations|realistic_example
1|Source verification|Checking whether a claimed origin is consistent with trusted evidence.|For IP traffic, validate source addresses against legitimate network prefixes; for messages, use authenticated channels and independently verified identities.|Choose the mechanism for the actual layer: network anti-spoofing and email identity verification are not interchangeable.|A plausible address or familiar display name is not proof. Local filtering cannot stop forged traffic sent by other networks.|An ISP rejects a customer packet claiming an external source prefix.
2|Authenticated encryption with associated data (AEAD)|Encryption that also authenticates the ciphertext and optional unencrypted associated data.|A keyed tag is verified before application plaintext is accepted, preventing undetected ciphertext modification when the construction is used correctly.|Use a reviewed AEAD construction with protected keys, correct nonce requirements and safe error handling.|Nonce misuse, implementation defects or an exposed key can defeat protection. Associated data is authenticated but not encrypted.|A service rejects an altered encrypted token instead of processing its modified contents.
3|Controlled security change|A governed process for making and verifying security-relevant system changes.|Record the reason, authorize the change, test a reviewed design, deploy safely and verify results with a rollback plan.|Useful for migrating away from a broken cipher or unsafe configuration once a concrete corrective design exists.|It is management support, not a cryptographic primitive or evidence that a chosen replacement is secure.|A team stages a tested encryption-library migration rather than patching a cipher ad hoc.
4|Disable Bluetooth discoverability|Stopping routine Bluetooth discovery advertisements when a device does not need to be found.|The device limits how it announces its availability for discovery and pairing.|Useful after legitimate pairing and where platform behavior permits connections without continual discovery.|Known addresses and vulnerable services may remain reachable. This does not patch Bluetooth or make the radio invisible.|A paired headset remains usable while a phone stops inviting new discovery.
5|Disable unused Bluetooth|Turning off a device's Bluetooth radio or service when it is unnecessary.|Removing the active communication path prevents Bluetooth exchanges while disabled.|Appropriate for devices whose current workflow does not require Bluetooth peripherals or data transfer.|It does not fix other radios or remove malware already installed; operating-system controls must actually disable the interface.|A kiosk with wired peripherals has Bluetooth disabled in its managed configuration.
6|Dedicated cloud hosting resources|Reducing resource sharing with other tenants through a specified isolation arrangement.|Dedicated hosts or other provider guarantees reduce co-residency on the protected hardware resource.|Relevant to a demonstrated shared-resource threat and sensitive workloads with an assessed hosting requirement.|A dedicated virtual instance may still share physical hardware. Host, firmware and workload leakage can remain.|A sensitive computation moves to an explicitly dedicated physical host after a cache-sharing risk assessment.
7|Migration from WEP to supported WPA3|Replacing obsolete WEP wireless security with a supported modern Wi-Fi security configuration.|Compatible clients and access points negotiate WPA3 authentication and encryption instead of WEP.|Requires an inventory, tested client compatibility and a plan to remove obsolete modes and unsafe fallback.|It does not stop radio jamming or compromised clients. An interim supported WPA2-AES configuration may be needed for assessed compatibility.|A warehouse retires WEP-only scanners before enabling a WPA3-only network.
8|Hypervisor hardening|Reducing weaknesses and exposed interfaces in the software and host that run virtual machines.|Patch the host, restrict management access, minimize emulated devices and isolate administrative functions.|Relevant to guest-to-host attack paths and multi-tenant isolation boundaries.|Unknown flaws, vulnerable host firmware and excessive device passthrough can remain. Guest isolation needs more than a configuration checklist.|A virtualization team removes unused virtual devices and limits host administration to a separate management network.
9|Post-quantum cryptography|Cryptographic algorithms designed to resist known attacks using sufficiently capable quantum computers.|Replace vulnerable public-key operations through supported standardized algorithms and protocol implementations.|Use an inventory of cryptographic dependencies and required confidentiality lifetime to plan tested migration.|Not every primitive has the same quantum exposure. Migration errors, weak implementations and compromised endpoints remain risks.|A long-term archive plans a supported post-quantum key-establishment transition with interoperability testing.
10|Authenticated secure communication channels|Communication that protects data in transit and authenticates the intended peer where configured.|Protocols such as appropriately validated TLS prevent undetected interception on their protected path.|Useful when both the endpoint identity and the transport security configuration are verified.|Encryption does not make a fraudulent caller honest or protect plaintext after an endpoint is compromised.|A help desk uses an authenticated portal rather than accepting sensitive requests through an unverified caller.
11|Independent callback verification|Confirming a voice request through a separately trusted contact route.|End the unexpected call and use a known directory, established number or approved support workflow.|Useful for requests involving credentials, money, recovery or sensitive information.|Caller ID and voice familiarity can be spoofed. A number provided by the suspicious caller is not independent evidence.|An employee verifies a supposed IT call through the organization's published help-desk extension.
12|Challenge-response authentication|An identity check in which a claimant answers a fresh challenge using an appropriate secret or possession factor.|The verifier checks a response bound to the challenge and rejects reused or invalid proofs.|Suitable when the protocol, factor and verification channel are designed to resist replay and impersonation.|Public trivia questions are weak. A verified identity still requires authorization for the requested action.|A registered authenticator signs a new server challenge during an approved identity check.
13|Privacy policies and handling rules|Statements and procedures describing how personal information is collected, used, shared and disposed of.|Operational rules can define permitted disclosures and disposal responsibilities; a public notice communicates those practices.|Relevant to information governance when procedures are implemented and enforced.|A notice does not authenticate a requester, shred a document or stop social engineering by itself.|A reception procedure prohibits disclosing staff contact details without a verified business purpose.
14|Secure paper shredding|Physical destruction that makes sensitive paper information impractical to reconstruct at the required risk level.|An appropriate shredder or vetted destruction service reduces paper to sufficiently small fragments with controlled custody.|Use for paper records at end of retention, with a method suited to their sensitivity.|Ordinary tearing may be insufficient. Drives and flash memory need technology-appropriate sanitization or destruction instead.|An office transfers confidential paper from locked collection bins to a verified destruction service.
15|Locked waste containers|Physical access control over discarded material before secure collection or destruction.|Locks, placement and collection procedures reduce unauthorized access to bins and disposal areas.|Useful during the interval between discarding material and its final destruction.|The contents remain recoverable if the container or collection chain is breached. Locking is not sanitization.|A data center uses locked document bins inside a restricted service area.
16|Screen privacy filters|A display accessory or feature that reduces readability from off-axis viewing angles.|The screen remains usable from the intended position while side views are darkened or obscured.|Useful for sensitive work in shared spaces along with suitable seating and awareness.|It does not block a directly positioned camera, protect keyboard entry or secure an unlocked session.|A traveling employee adds a privacy filter and avoids opening sensitive documents near an overlooking seat.
17|Biometric authentication|Using measured physical or behavioral characteristics as part of identity verification.|A sensor compares a captured characteristic with an enrolled reference under an appropriate authentication design.|Use with protected templates, presentation-attack controls and suitable fallback; local biometrics often unlock a device-bound credential.|Biometrics are not easily replaced secrets and do not automatically authenticate a remote caller. Compromised endpoints can still misuse sessions.|A workstation uses a fingerprint to unlock a device-bound sign-in key rather than a reusable typed password.
18|Privacy and permission settings|Platform controls limiting which applications or parties can access personal data and sensors.|Restrict unnecessary permissions, tracking features, audience visibility and data-sharing defaults.|Useful before granting access and during periodic application permission review.|A privileged malicious program may bypass these controls; a setting is not a substitute for malware removal.|A mobile application loses microphone and location access when those capabilities are not needed.
19|Anti-spyware protection|Endpoint detection and removal focused on unauthorized monitoring software.|Recognize known spyware or suspicious collection behavior, then quarantine and investigate affected components.|Useful with current detection information, appropriate privileges and a response process.|Novel, privileged or concealed surveillance can evade detection. Already stolen information and credentials require separate response.|A managed endpoint quarantines a program that records browsing activity without authorization.
20|Time-based one-time authentication codes|Short-lived authentication values derived from a secret and a time interval.|The verifier checks codes within a limited window, with replay controls and rate limiting.|Useful as an additional authentication factor when secrets and recovery processes are protected.|Codes can be phished or relayed in real time, and may be replayable inside the accepted interval if not tracked.|A user signs in with a password and an authenticator code, while the service rejects code reuse.
21|Authenticated nonces and freshness checks|Unique values used to bind an operation to a fresh protocol exchange.|Include a nonce in the authenticated message and verify its expected value or non-reuse at the receiver.|Useful for replay-resistant authentication and device commands with adequate state or challenge handling.|A random value that is neither authenticated nor checked does not prevent replay. Reset and concurrency behavior need design.|A controller rejects an old signed unlock response because it does not match the current challenge.
22|Trusted acquisition sources|Obtaining software or information through an independently established and verified source.|Check publisher identity, distribution route and integrity rather than accepting a convenient download mirror.|Useful for reducing fake installers and misleading advice in everyday workflows.|Reputable publishers and their build pipelines can be compromised. Familiarity and search ranking do not prove integrity.|An administrator retrieves an installer from the known vendor portal and verifies its release signature.
23|Browser advertising blockers|Tools that filter selected advertising requests or page elements.|Request rules and content filters reduce exposure to unwanted advertising and some malicious advertising paths.|Useful within supported browsers where rules and extension permissions are managed responsibly.|They do not uninstall every adware application, detect all malware or guarantee a permitted site is safe.|A browser blocks advertising requests on a site while endpoint staff investigate a bundled advertising program.
24|File and system integrity monitoring|Comparing protected resources with trusted expected state to detect unauthorized changes.|Record baselines and alert on relevant file, configuration or boot-state differences using protected evidence.|Useful where changes can be distinguished from authorized maintenance and alerts are investigated.|An attacker controlling the monitor or baseline can hide changes. A rootkit may deceive tools running inside its own compromised OS.|A monitored server alerts when a protected system binary changes outside the maintenance window.
25|Secure Boot and startup trust|A boot-chain mechanism that verifies permitted boot components before loading them.|Firmware trust policy checks signatures or allowed measurements for the relevant startup code.|Useful for reducing unauthorized bootloaders and some boot-level persistence when keys and configuration are managed.|Signed vulnerable components, compromised firmware and post-boot attacks remain concerns. It is not proof that the whole running OS is clean.|A workstation refuses an unsigned replacement bootloader after an attempted bootkit installation.
26|Process memory inspection|Examining active memory for malicious code, injected regions or suspicious state.|Endpoint tools inspect executable mappings, process relationships and memory artifacts unavailable to file-only scans.|Useful for memory-resident activity when tooling has sufficient access and evidence is collected safely.|Protected processes, evasion and short-lived activity can limit visibility. Memory collection can expose sensitive data and needs controls.|An analyst identifies injected executable memory in a normally trusted process.
27|Behavioral security analytics|Analysis of sequences and relationships of system activity to identify suspicious behavior.|Correlate process, identity and network events instead of relying solely on a matching malicious file hash.|Useful for fileless execution and abuse of legitimate tools when telemetry is sufficiently complete.|Unusual behavior can be legitimate and common behavior can be malicious. Alerts need investigation and tuning.|A script interpreter spawning an unexpected credential-access tool triggers a correlated alert.
28|Radio frequency hopping|A supported communication method that changes radio channels according to an agreed scheme.|Channel diversity can reduce disruption from interference occupying only part of the available spectrum.|Relevant to compatible radio systems and assessed narrow-band interference within regulatory limits.|It does not defeat all jamming, authenticate users or replace encryption; wideband interference can affect every hop.|A supported industrial radio link moves among permitted channels while an alternate communication path is prepared.
29|Protected and tested backups|Independent data copies and recovery procedures for restoring information after loss or corruption.|Keep recoverable versions with isolation or immutability, separate credentials and regular restore testing.|Useful for ransomware recovery, accidental deletion and other data-loss events.|Backups do not stop infection or undo exfiltration. A copy writable through compromised credentials may be encrypted too.|A recovery team restores a clean file-server version from immutable storage after containing ransomware.
30|Environmental monitoring sensors|Instruments that measure physical conditions affecting systems or facilities.|Temperature, water, smoke or power sensors report values and trigger actionable alerts.|Useful where thresholds, calibration, response responsibility and communications are maintained.|An alert does not repair the equipment or prove deliberate interference. Sensors can fail or be positioned badly.|A water detector beneath cooling equipment alerts operators before leakage reaches a rack.
31|Operational redundancy|Additional components or service paths that preserve availability when one fails.|Replicate critical capability and test failover with sufficiently independent failure domains.|Useful for power, network or service failures when alternate capacity is actually usable.|Shared dependencies, replicated bad configuration and correlated attacks can disable both paths. Redundancy is not a data backup.|A service fails over to an independent power and network path during a facility incident.
32|Cost-tuned password hashing|Storing password verification data using a suitable salted password-derivation function rather than a fast general hash.|A deliberately expensive computation raises the cost of each offline candidate, with unique salts preventing shared precomputation.|Use an appropriate current KDF, measured resource costs and a migration strategy for older hashes.|Weak passwords can still be guessed; excessive cost can overload the service. A generic strong hash such as plain SHA is not sufficient.|A password service upgrades verified logins to a current cost-tuned password-hash format.
33|Security configuration and access audits|Structured examination of controls, permissions and configuration against an intended baseline.|Inspect effective access and evidence, record deviations and track remediation to verification.|Useful for finding public cloud exposure, excessive privileges and drift in deployed environments.|An audit is a point-in-time or scheduled check, not automatic enforcement. Findings do not fix themselves.|A storage audit identifies an unintended anonymous-read policy and verifies its subsequent removal.
34|Authenticated data encryption|Cryptographic protection of data confidentiality with integrity and identity checks appropriate to the context.|Use suitable protocols or storage constructions and protect keys at trusted endpoints.|Useful against unauthorized reading or modification of data on a protected path or medium.|Encryption at rest does not override public read authorization. Traffic metadata and compromised endpoints can remain exposed.|A captured application session reveals encrypted payloads but still shows packet sizes and destination addresses.
35|Supported software removal tools|Installation-management or remediation tools that remove unwanted applications and related components.|Identify packages, uninstall safely and verify that associated services or persistence are removed.|Useful for unnecessary software on a trusted system or as part of a defined malware response.|Some removals break dependencies; malware can survive incomplete cleanup. Privileged compromise may require rebuilding.|An endpoint administrator removes unused trial software from a fleet image and checks remaining services.
36|Clean system rebuilding|Reinstalling a system from a verified trusted image and restoring only assessed data and configuration.|Replace potentially compromised operating-system state instead of trying to identify every altered component.|Useful after serious compromise when trust cannot be restored by selective removal.|Restoring infected backups, compromised firmware or stolen credentials can reintroduce the problem. Preserve required evidence first.|A server is rebuilt from a controlled image after root compromise, with credentials replaced separately.
37|Operational security monitoring|Collecting and examining events to identify conditions requiring investigation or response.|Use relevant logs, network observations and alerts with defined ownership and retention.|Useful for brand abuse, compromised devices and other observable security activity.|Monitoring does not automatically prevent an event, and missing or tampered telemetry creates blind spots.|A team correlates unexpected outbound connections with endpoint alerts before isolating a suspected bot.
38|Security awareness education|Instruction that helps people recognize risks and follow workable protective practices.|Use relevant examples, reporting routes and reinforcement suited to actual job decisions.|Useful for phishing, suspicious downloads and deceptive domains alongside technical safeguards.|Education cannot guarantee that every person detects every attack. Poorly designed processes can defeat even trained staff.|A workshop shows how to verify a vendor URL using a known bookmark rather than a search advertisement.
39|Input and schema validation|Checking untrusted data against an explicit expected structure and allowed values.|Apply server-side type, length, format and business-rule checks before using input.|Useful for APIs, event data and command parameters when rules reflect the true operation.|Syntactic validity does not prove authenticity or authorization. Validation alone does not replace parameterized SQL or context-safe output handling.|A function rejects an event whose resource identifier is outside its documented format.
40|Authentication assertion checks|Verifying that an identity claim is genuine and valid for the intended context.|Check protocol-specific signatures, issuer, audience, expiration and freshness before creating a session.|Useful when services accept authentication tokens, federated assertions or signed requests.|Authentication does not automatically authorize every requested action. Checking only token syntax or an algorithm label is insufficient.|A relying service rejects a signed token issued for a different application audience.
41|Independent request verification|Checking a claimed fact or requested action against separately trusted evidence.|Confirm identity, intent and relevant details through known channels or approved records.|Useful for financial changes, support requests and software release decisions when the procedure is specific.|A vague instruction to verify is not a technical control until the evidence and procedure are defined.|Accounts payable confirms a bank-detail change using the supplier's established phone number.
42|Social-engineering response training|Practice in recognizing manipulation and using safe challenge and escalation procedures.|Teach staff to pause, verify and report requests despite urgency, rewards or apparent authority.|Useful for pretexting and quid-pro-quo approaches with practical support from management.|Training does not authenticate a requester or block malware. Staff need a process they can actually follow under pressure.|A help-desk exercise rehearses refusing password requests while offering a legitimate recovery route.
43|Visitor escort procedures|Rules requiring an authorized sponsor to accompany and account for visitors in controlled areas.|Define access scope, check-in, supervision and safe escalation when a visitor separates from the escort.|Useful for visitors whose limited authorization does not permit independent movement.|A written rule without staffing and compliance does not enforce access. Emergency safety and accessibility remain essential.|A contractor is escorted between the reception desk and an approved equipment room.
44|Configured WPA3 wireless security|Modern Wi-Fi authentication and encryption using the appropriate WPA3 personal or enterprise configuration.|SAE or enterprise authentication protects association under the selected deployment model, with required management protections.|Use compatible patched clients and access points and an assessed transition configuration.|WPA3 does not patch every implementation flaw, prevent jamming or certify endpoint integrity. Unsafe fallback can weaken a deployment.|An enterprise distributes verified authentication-server settings to managed wireless clients.
45|Device firmware security updates|Vendor-supported changes that repair known defects in embedded device software.|Verify update provenance, assess compatibility, deploy and confirm the required version and configuration.|Useful when an applicable fix exists for an access point, camera or other supported device.|Updates can disrupt service and do not guarantee removal of prior compromise. Unsupported devices may need replacement.|A tested access-point firmware update closes a documented implementation weakness.
46|Request rate limiting|Restricting request frequency or resource use over a defined interval.|Enforce appropriate quotas before expensive work, using identities or other assessed grouping keys.|Useful for login guessing and application-resource exhaustion when enforcement occurs at the relevant boundary.|Distributed sources, shared addresses and expensive permitted requests complicate limits. Local limits cannot create upstream bandwidth.|An API rejects excessive per-token requests before allocating costly backend jobs.
47|Load balancing and capacity distribution|Spreading service traffic across multiple healthy instances or locations.|Routing and health checks distribute work and support failover across available capacity.|Useful for legitimate demand, instance failures and some resource-exhaustion events.|An attacker can overwhelm shared dependencies or total capacity. A load balancer does not itself distinguish malicious requests.|A service shifts traffic away from a failing instance while maintaining capacity in another zone.
48|Mutual peer authentication|A protocol arrangement in which both communicating endpoints prove their identities.|Each party verifies a credential bound to the intended peer and session before trusting application data.|Useful for device-to-service control channels and inter-service communication.|Trust-anchor errors, stolen keys and missing identity checks can defeat it. Encryption alone is not mutual authentication.|A controller accepts commands only after validating the authorized device certificate and server identity.
49|SHA-3 hashing|A standardized family of cryptographic hash and extendable-output functions.|A selected variant produces a digest for a specified security purpose without a shared secret.|Useful for appropriate integrity or cryptographic constructions when the variant and output size meet the requirement.|An unkeyed digest does not authenticate its sender. SHA-3 alone is not a password-storage scheme or a fix for every collision-sensitive design.|A protocol uses a reviewed SHA-3 variant as one component of its specified construction.
50|Adequate cryptographic output length|Choosing an output size that supplies the required work factor for the particular primitive and threat.|For ideal n-bit hashes, generic collision search is about 2^(n/2), distinct from an n-bit preimage target.|Useful when designing identifiers or hashes that actually require collision resistance.|Length does not rescue a broken algorithm, poor randomness or an unsuitable protocol. Password strength is a separate property.|A toy short digest is replaced with an appropriately sized modern hash for document integrity checks.
51|BCP 38 source-address filtering|Network boundary filtering intended to reduce traffic with forged source IP addresses.|Reject outgoing or incoming packets whose claimed source is inconsistent with the legitimate connected network.|Useful at networks that originate spoofed reflection requests, with routing-aware deployment.|It does not stop every DDoS technique or filter forged packets originating outside the enforcing network.|A provider filters customer egress so a compromised host cannot claim arbitrary victim addresses.
52|Network firewall rules|Explicit policy deciding which network flows or packets may cross a boundary.|Apply permitted addresses, services, connection states or application-aware conditions according to the firewall capability.|Useful for restricting unnecessary exposure and dropping identifiable unwanted traffic.|Overly broad rules, encrypted traffic and allowed compromised services limit effectiveness. Local filtering cannot repair a saturated upstream link.|An edge policy blocks an unnecessary exposed UDP service used in reflection abuse.
53|Dynamic ARP Inspection|Switch validation of local ARP messages against trusted IP/MAC bindings.|Use verified bindings, commonly DHCP-snooping data and approved exceptions, to reject inconsistent advertisements.|Useful on supported managed local networks with carefully configured trust ports.|Bad bindings or trust settings can interrupt legitimate hosts. It does not authenticate arbitrary traffic beyond the local link.|A switch rejects an ARP claim that conflicts with the gateway's trusted binding.
54|Static network address bindings|Manually fixed address associations instead of learning them dynamically.|Pin selected IP-to-MAC mappings or related entries on appropriate devices.|Useful in small stable networks where changes can be managed and verified reliably.|Operational drift and spoofed or compromised legitimate devices remain risks. Large-scale manual maintenance is error prone.|A small control network uses managed static neighbor entries for critical peers.
55|DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC)|A system of signatures and trust relationships that authenticates DNS data for signed zones.|A validating resolver verifies the chain of trust and signatures before accepting protected responses.|Useful against forged or modified DNS answers when signing and validation are correctly deployed.|It does not encrypt queries, secure unsigned data automatically or prove the destination website is benign.|A resolver refuses an altered response whose signature cannot be validated to the trusted DNS chain.
56|Hardened and trusted DNS resolvers|Resolvers configured to resist abuse, validate appropriate data and restrict administrative and recursive access.|Patch resolver software, limit recursion where appropriate and use validation and protected management.|Useful for reducing cache poisoning and resolver misuse within an assessed name-resolution design.|Selecting a familiar resolver is not proof of DNSSEC validation or protection from every registrar or authoritative-server compromise.|An organization operates patched validating resolvers with recursion limited to its intended clients.
57|Context-specific escaping|Representing special characters so a specific interpreter treats them as literal data.|Apply the correct library and rules for the actual interpreter and encoding context.|Useful only where avoiding that interpreter or using separated arguments is not possible.|Shell quoting can be fragile and may not prevent argument injection. SQL, HTML and shell escaping are not interchangeable.|A legacy integration uses a reviewed escaping library while being migrated away from shell command construction.
58|Allowlisting permitted operations|Restricting accepted inputs, commands or identities to a defined approved set.|Reject values outside a small, explicit server-side policy and avoid arbitrary interpreter construction.|Useful when a service should offer only documented operations or controlled parameter forms.|An overly broad allowlist or unsafe downstream interpretation can still be exploitable. The approved set needs maintenance.|An administrative API accepts only enumerated maintenance operation names and uses library calls to execute them.
59|Constant-time cryptographic implementation|Code designed to avoid leaking secrets through data-dependent execution behavior relevant to the platform.|Use reviewed primitives without secret-dependent branches or memory access in sensitive paths.|Useful against certain timing and cache side channels in comparisons and cryptographic operations.|Scheduling noise does not establish constant-time behavior; compiler, hardware, power and fault channels require separate analysis.|A token verifier uses a suitable constant-time comparison rather than exiting on the first differing byte.
60|Hardware security module (HSM)|A dedicated cryptographic device or service boundary that protects keys and performs permitted operations.|Keys can remain inside the module while access policy, auditing and protected cryptographic operations constrain use.|Useful when an assessed security boundary and key-custody requirement justify the module's assurance level.|A module may still be misconfigured or vulnerable; authorized misuse and side channels are not automatically eliminated.|A signing service requests a signature from an HSM without exporting the private key to application memory.
61|Independent code review|Examination of changes for security defects, unintended behavior and policy violations before acceptance.|Reviewers inspect sensitive paths, tests, dependencies and change intent using an accountable process.|Useful for dangerous triggers, insecure APIs and privileged code when reviewers understand the context.|Review can miss obfuscation, large changes or compromised build artifacts. Reviewing source alone does not prove the shipped binary matches.|A second maintainer rejects a destructive scheduled task hidden in a routine maintenance change.
62|Resource access controls|Policies that limit who or what may perform particular operations on resources.|Enforce authenticated identity, permissions and appropriate contextual rules at each relevant boundary.|Useful for limiting data access, destructive changes and unauthorized use of privileged functions.|An authorized account can still misuse permitted actions, and missing checks on a second path can bypass policy.|Only an approved service identity may modify a sensitive deployment configuration.
63|Authenticated timestamps and freshness windows|Time values used with trusted clocks and protocol checks to constrain message age.|Authenticate the timestamp and reject messages outside a defined window, usually with additional replay tracking.|Useful when devices can maintain appropriate time and tolerate a designed acceptance window.|A timestamp alone permits repeated messages within the window. Clock drift and rollback handling require design.|A device rejects a signed command that is too old and tracks accepted message identifiers within the allowed interval.
64|User behavior analytics|Analysis of identity activity to identify behavior inconsistent with established patterns or expectations.|Correlate logins, data access, privilege use and movement across systems for investigation.|Useful for detecting some insider abuse and compromised accounts with adequate contextual telemetry.|An anomaly is not proof of malicious intent; false negatives, privacy concerns and baseline drift require oversight.|An employee account's unusual bulk export triggers a review of business justification.
65|Least privilege|Granting only the permissions and duration needed for a specific task.|Remove excess standing rights and separate routine work from controlled elevation.|Useful for users, services, cloud functions and administrative paths to limit opportunity and impact.|It does not fix every software flaw or stop misuse of legitimately needed rights. Effective permissions must be tested.|A reporting service can read its own dataset but cannot administer the database server.
66|Data Execution Prevention (DEP)|A memory-protection mechanism that prevents execution from pages not marked executable.|Processor and operating-system page protections enforce executable versus non-executable memory regions.|Useful as one layer against injected code in data pages and some memory-corruption exploitation.|It does not stop every memory write or code-reuse attack; legitimate executable mappings and other bypass paths remain.|A process terminates when an exploit tries to execute injected bytes from a non-executable data page.
67|Secure software construction|Engineering practices that remove security defects through appropriate design, APIs, languages and verification.|Use memory-safe abstractions, explicit authorization, separated data and commands, tests and review appropriate to the threat.|Useful for preventing root causes rather than relying only on exploit mitigations.|The label is broad: each defect needs a concrete practice and verification. A coding checklist cannot prove absence of flaws.|A parser replaces an unchecked copy with a bounds-safe interface and tests oversized inputs.
68|Mutual-exclusion locking (mutex)|A synchronization mechanism that lets only one participating execution path enter a protected critical section.|Acquire the shared lock before reading and updating the related state, then release it safely.|Useful when all paths obey the same lock discipline and the protected invariant is correctly identified.|Locks can deadlock or fail across non-participating processes; locking one variable may not protect a multi-resource invariant.|Threads serialize updates to a shared account balance through one agreed lock.
69|Atomic operations and transactions|Operations whose relevant effects are indivisible to competing participants under the stated consistency model.|Use suitable atomic instructions or transactions to protect an entire security-relevant state transition.|Useful for preventing check-and-update races when the operation spans the required invariant.|Atomicity of individual reads does not make a compound workflow safe. Distributed transactions and isolation levels need separate design.|A database transaction verifies and deducts an available balance as one protected operation.
70|Anti-malware protection|Tools that detect, block or remediate malicious software using signatures and other techniques.|Inspect files, processes and behavior, then quarantine or trigger investigation under managed policy.|Useful where the tool has sufficient visibility and current detection capability.|Unknown, privileged or evasive malware can be missed. A clean scan does not prove that all compromise effects are removed.|An endpoint blocks a recognized credential-stealing program and alerts the response team.
71|On-screen virtual keyboards|Input interfaces that let users select characters without typing them through a physical keyboard.|They may bypass a simple hardware or software logger that captures only physical key events.|Useful only against a clearly defined input interception path and with appropriate accessibility design.|Screen capture, mouse recording and browser hooks can still capture input. They are not a general defense against a compromised endpoint.|A special-purpose kiosk avoids a simple hardware keystroke recorder but still requires tamper inspection.
72|Session-cookie security attributes|Browser cookie controls that limit exposure of session identifiers.|Secure restricts transport, HttpOnly limits script access and appropriate SameSite settings constrain cross-site sending.|Useful alongside HTTPS, safe session generation, rotation, invalidation and XSS prevention.|These attributes have different purposes; none makes a stolen valid token universally unusable.|A login service issues its session cookie with appropriate transport and script-access restrictions.
73|Session expiration and idle timeouts|Ending session validity after a defined period of inactivity or total lifetime.|The server checks lifetime and invalidates the session rather than relying only on a browser display timer.|Useful for reducing the window of abandoned or stolen session use, with limits suitable for the application.|An attacker can act before expiration; idle sessions may be kept active. Timeouts do not remove endpoint malware.|A financial application requires reauthentication after an appropriate idle interval.
74|Security-claim source verification|Checking a warning, instruction or incident claim against independently trusted evidence.|Consult genuine vendor advisories, internal security staff and original evidence rather than forwarding rumors.|Useful for hoaxes and misleading security instructions before taking disruptive action.|A trusted source can be mistaken or outdated; verify applicability and context rather than treating reputation as proof.|Staff verify an alarming deletion instruction with the security team before touching system files.
75|Incident response plans|Prepared responsibilities and procedures for identifying, containing, investigating and recovering from incidents.|Define escalation routes, evidence handling, communications and tested recovery decisions.|Useful when personnel exercise the plan and have authority and resources to act.|A document alone neither detects nor blocks an incident. Rigid procedures can fail when the real situation differs.|An exercised response plan coordinates containment and communications during a suspected ransomware incident.
76|Prepared SQL statements and bound parameters|Database interfaces that keep supplied values separate from SQL program structure.|The driver binds data values to a fixed query rather than concatenating them into executable SQL.|Useful for database access paths that support parameterization and appropriately scoped database permissions.|Identifiers and dynamic query structure require controlled selection; parameterizing one path does not fix another unsafe path.|A search query binds the user's search term through a parameter rather than inserting it into SQL text.
77|Web application firewall (WAF)|A control that inspects HTTP traffic and applies application-focused filtering policy.|Rules or behavioral checks identify and block selected malicious request patterns before the application handles them.|Useful as a supplementary layer, including temporary mitigation while an application defect is repaired.|Bypasses, false positives, protocol differences and encrypted visibility limits remain. It does not replace safe application code.|A WAF rule blocks a known exploit request while developers repair the vulnerable endpoint.
78|Request-bound anti-CSRF tokens|Unpredictable values used to distinguish legitimate state-changing browser requests from cross-site forgeries.|Generate and validate tokens bound to the appropriate session or request context on the server.|Useful for applications that otherwise rely on automatically sent credentials such as cookies.|XSS, token leakage and missing validation undermine the design. A generic bearer token is not automatically an anti-CSRF token.|A profile-change form is rejected when its request lacks the expected session-bound anti-CSRF value.
79|SameSite cookie policy|Browser rules controlling cookie transmission in cross-site request contexts.|Choose an appropriate Strict, Lax or other supported policy and verify behavior for the application's flows.|Useful as one layer against some CSRF and cross-site cookie exposure.|It is not a complete CSRF defense, especially for same-site attacks or unsafe endpoint design. Compatibility and necessary cross-site workflows matter.|A site tests that a cross-site navigation cannot silently perform a sensitive state-changing operation.
80|Account lockouts and progressive login restrictions|Controls limiting repeated authentication failures against an account.|Temporarily restrict attempts or apply increasing delays with appropriate recovery and monitoring.|Useful against online password guessing where the service controls the authentication path.|Attackers can abuse lockouts for denial of service, and low-rate spraying may evade per-account thresholds. Offline guesses are unaffected.|A portal slows repeated failed attempts and provides a protected recovery route for legitimate users.
81|CAPTCHA and automation challenges|Tests intended to make automated interaction more costly or difficult.|Present an assessed challenge when risk warrants it, using accessible alternatives and server-side verification.|Useful as supplementary friction in some automated-abuse workflows.|Challenges can be solved, relayed or bypassed and can exclude users. They are not a sole authentication or brute-force defense.|A signup service challenges suspicious automation while also enforcing quotas and abuse monitoring.
82|Strong password selection|Choosing long, unique passwords and rejecting commonly used or compromised candidates.|Use adequate length, blocklists and password managers rather than relying on predictable character substitutions.|Useful against likely-password guessing for secrets that must still be user-entered.|Forced composition recipes can encourage predictable patterns. Better selection does not prevent phishing or theft from an endpoint.|A user creates a unique generated password instead of a sports team name with a year suffix.
83|Per-password salting|Adding a unique salt as input to the password-hashing process.|Store the salt with the verifier so identical passwords do not share a reusable precomputed hash computation.|Useful with a suitable cost-tuned password KDF for stored verification records.|Salts are not secrets and do not block online guesses or make an individual weak password unguessable.|Two accounts using the same password have distinct stored verification values because their salts differ.
84|Correlated failed-login monitoring|Collecting and analyzing failed authentication events across accounts, sources and time.|Detect patterns such as many accounts receiving a few common guesses, not only repeated failures on one account.|Useful for spotting spraying and other online authentication abuse with adequate retention and response.|It is detection, not automatic prevention. Attackers may distribute activity or blend with ordinary user failures.|A security alert links low-rate failures across hundreds of accounts to a coordinated spraying campaign.
85|Multi-factor authentication (MFA)|Authentication using independent factors rather than repeated versions of the same secret type.|Require an additional factor and protect enrollment, recovery and factor replacement; prefer phishing-resistant designs where appropriate.|Useful for reducing takeover using a stolen password alone.|Some methods can be phished or fatigued. MFA does not protect every existing session or cure a compromised endpoint.|A service requires a device-bound authenticator proof that a stolen password alone cannot provide.
86|Uniquely salted password verifiers|Stored password hashes computed using a different salt for each password record.|Per-record salts defeat shared lookup tables and reveal less about identical user passwords.|Use with an appropriate slow or memory-hard KDF and protected migration procedures.|A salted fast hash can still be guessed rapidly. Salting is not encryption and does not stop online credential stuffing.|A legacy application migrates unsalted hashes to a versioned salted password-verifier format.
87|Password KDF work factors (key stretching)|Deliberately expensive derivation that increases the cost of testing each password guess.|Apply a suitable KDF with measured CPU and, where appropriate, memory cost and unique salts.|Useful against offline guessing when parameters balance security and authentication capacity.|It does not add entropy to a weak password or prevent online attacks by itself. Excessive cost can create service-exhaustion risks.|A password service periodically reassesses its KDF cost as hardware and operational capacity change.
88|Credential breach monitoring|Checking for evidence that organizational accounts or credentials have appeared in known exposures.|Correlate reliable breach intelligence with accounts and trigger verification, reset or investigation as appropriate.|Useful for finding reuse exposure and initiating response after a third-party breach.|Coverage is incomplete and reports can be delayed or wrong. Monitoring does not revoke a credential by itself.|A confirmed exposure leads the identity team to invalidate affected secrets and check suspicious sign-ins.
89|Unique passwords per service|Using a distinct password for each account so one service's exposure does not unlock another.|A password manager can generate and retain separate strong secrets rather than relying on human memorization of variations.|Useful for limiting cross-service credential stuffing after another service exposes reusable passwords.|It does not protect a credential stolen from the very service being attacked or a compromised password manager or endpoint.|A retail password leak cannot authenticate to the user's email account because its password is different.
90|Secure initial and compromised-credential changes|Requiring changes when defaults, exposure or recovery make an existing secret unsafe.|Provision unique initial credentials, disable shared defaults and replace known compromised credentials through verified workflows.|Useful before device deployment and after evidence of exposure.|Arbitrary periodic changes without evidence are not a universal best practice and can encourage weak patterns. Rotation alone does not repair an active compromise.|A device cannot enter production until its published factory administrator credential is removed.
91|System hardening|Reducing unnecessary services, unsafe defaults and excess administrative exposure.|Apply a reviewed baseline, remove unused functions and verify effective configuration after deployment.|Useful for reducing default-password exposure and avoidable attack surface.|A hardened baseline can drift and does not patch every unknown vulnerability. Hardening must respect operational needs.|An appliance's remote administration is restricted and unused accounts and services are disabled.
92|Disable obsolete protocols and features|Removing deprecated negotiation options and legacy access paths that the system no longer needs.|Configure services to reject unsupported versions, weak algorithms or unsafe fallback, then test required clients.|Useful when older options enable downgrade or insecure authentication paths.|Compatibility dependencies need assessment; disabling an option does not fix every vulnerability in the remaining implementation.|A service stops accepting an obsolete transport version after all required clients are tested.
93|HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS)|A browser policy directing future access to an origin through HTTPS and disallowing certain insecure fallbacks.|The site sends a policy over a valid HTTPS connection; approved preload deployment can cover an initial visit.|Useful against some HTTP downgrade and cookie transport exposure in supporting browsers.|It does not authenticate a look-alike domain or repair every TLS version downgrade. Initial-visit and subdomain policy need planning.|A browser refuses to downgrade a known HSTS site to plain HTTP on a hostile network.
94|Wireless intrusion detection and prevention|Monitoring wireless infrastructure for unauthorized or suspicious activity and applying assessed response policies.|Compare observed radios and configurations with approved inventory and investigate anomalies.|Useful for discovering rogue or impersonating access points with adequate sensor coverage.|False positives and blind spots exist; automatic disruption may cause outages or legal issues. Detection is not proof of malicious intent.|Wireless sensors locate an unapproved access point connected to an office network.
95|MAC-address filtering|Allowing or denying local network participation based on observed link-layer addresses.|Compare a device's claimed MAC address with a configured list at a supported access point or switch.|Useful for limited administrative inventory controls when stronger authentication is also present.|MAC addresses are observable and spoofable. This does not authenticate an access point or reliably prevent a rogue AP.|A small isolated lab uses MAC lists as an inventory aid, not as its primary device identity control.
96|High-security mechanical locks|Locks designed and assessed for specified resistance to manipulation and unauthorized opening.|Use an appropriate cylinder, protected installation and controlled key management as part of the door assembly.|Useful where mechanical access resistance is required and the selected assurance level matches the risk.|No lock is invulnerable; weak frames, copied keys and alternate entrances may bypass the cylinder.|A restricted store installs a rated cylinder and tracks authorized key issuance.
97|Electronic physical access security|Electronic systems controlling or recording physical entry and related security events.|Readers, door controllers, sensors and audit records enforce a defined access policy and support response.|Useful with reliable power, safe egress, protected credentials and maintained equipment.|A door reader does not necessarily strengthen a mechanical lock or prevent tailgating. Controllers themselves require security.|An entry controller logs a door opening and checks the presented credential against current access rights.
98|Cryptographically authenticated RFID|RFID credentials using protected cryptographic operations rather than relying only on a readable static identifier.|Challenge-response can prove possession of a tag secret while freshness checks reject simple recording and replay.|Useful when readers, credentials and key management support an assessed authentication design.|Encrypted stored data alone may still be clonable; relay attacks and compromised readers require additional consideration.|A replacement badge system rejects a copied serial number because the counterfeit cannot answer its fresh challenge.
99|Radio shielding|Materials or enclosures that reduce radio transmission through a physical boundary.|Attenuate the relevant frequencies with appropriate coverage, installation and tested effectiveness.|Useful for limiting unintended RFID reads or reducing particular electromagnetic exposure.|Gaps, unsupported frequencies and handling outside the enclosure remain risks. Shielding does not make a cloned identifier unforgeable.|Sensitive contactless credentials are kept in a tested shielded holder when not in use.
100|Identity and access management (IAM)|Systems and policies managing identities, credentials and their authorized access to resources.|Issue, authenticate, authorize, review and revoke identities and permissions through defined lifecycle controls.|Useful for people, services and cloud functions when effective permissions are assessed rather than assumed.|An authenticated or validly assigned identity can still be overprivileged or compromised. Policy interactions can be complex.|A function receives a service identity allowed to read only its assigned storage location.
101|Authenticated sequence numbers|Ordered message identifiers used with verification state to reject duplicates or out-of-window messages.|Authenticate the sequence field and maintain an appropriate acceptance window for the session.|Useful for replay protection in command and transport protocols.|Unauthenticated numbering can be altered; resets, wraparound and state loss can reopen replay paths.|A controller discards an authenticated command number it has already processed.
102|One-time authentication passwords or codes|Values intended to be accepted only once for a defined authentication purpose.|Bind a generated code to the intended operation and invalidate it after use with server-side state or protocol checks.|Useful for specific authentication or recovery flows with protected delivery and verification.|Codes can be stolen or relayed before use; time-limited is not automatically single-use. They do not protect arbitrary messages without binding.|A recovery code becomes invalid immediately after a verified password-recovery transaction.
103|Email threat filtering|Inspection of message attributes and content to reduce delivery of harmful mail.|Evaluate attachments, URLs, sender signals and malicious patterns with quarantine and reporting workflows.|Useful at mail gateways and endpoints when rules and detection data are maintained.|Novel lures and compromised senders can pass checks; false positives require review. Delivery is not a trust certification.|A gateway quarantines a recognized malicious attachment before it reaches an employee mailbox.
104|User security training|Role-appropriate instruction for recognizing unsafe requests and following verified workflows.|Teach concrete actions and reporting paths using relevant examples and reinforcement.|Useful as a human layer alongside usable technical and organizational controls.|Training does not guarantee recognition or excuse unsafe system design. Outcomes should not depend on perfect vigilance.|Staff practice checking a document-sharing request through the known service rather than an unexpected link.
105|Message-sender verification|Checking a sender's identity and request using appropriate technical evidence and trusted channels.|Inspect authenticated domain signals and confirm sensitive requests independently when risk warrants it.|Useful for targeted messages that exploit familiarity or current business details.|A compromised legitimate mailbox can pass domain checks. Authentic sender identity does not prove benign intent.|An engineer confirms a sensitive document request with the known project lead through an established channel.
106|Managed threat-protection controls|A broad category of controls that identify or block malicious content and activity.|Specify the actual product capability, telemetry, prevention policy and response process rather than relying on the label.|Useful when a concrete threat path and supported detection or blocking function are identified.|The term is not a standardized guarantee. Unknown attacks, missing visibility and configuration gaps can defeat coverage.|A mail-and-endpoint deployment links a malicious attachment alert to containment of the affected workstation.
107|Security simulation exercises|Controlled practice of security decisions and response procedures.|Use authorized scenarios with defined learning goals, safe execution and constructive feedback.|Useful for rehearsing phishing reporting, verification and incident escalation before a real event.|A simulation does not authenticate a real request or prove future immunity. Exercises must not harm operations or shame individuals.|A team rehearses independent verification of a tailored supplier-payment request.
108|Executive-focused security training|Training adapted to high-value roles, authority pressure and sensitive business decisions.|Practice delegation, payment verification and secure handling of board or acquisition material with relevant staff.|Useful for executives and assistants targeted by tailored deception.|Role-specific training does not override the need for technical controls or independent transaction checks.|An executive assistant practices challenging an urgent request that claims normal approvals must be skipped.
109|Out-of-band verification procedures|Documented steps for confirming a sensitive request through evidence independent of its delivery channel.|Use established contact information, dual approval or separately authenticated records before accepting changed instructions.|Useful for transfers, credential recovery and privileged access changes.|A second channel controlled by the same attacker is not independent. Procedures need exceptions that cannot silently bypass verification.|Finance confirms altered supplier bank details using the supplier's pre-existing verified contact record.
110|Restricted access to sensitive information|Limiting access to valuable data according to verified business needs.|Apply resource-specific permissions, review grants and log sensitive retrieval or export.|Useful for reducing the reach of a compromised or deceived executive or other user.|It does not establish whether a communication is genuine or stop misuse of legitimately accessible data.|A transaction team limits acquisition documents to approved participants instead of a broad executive group.
111|Independent navigation instead of unexpected text links|A user practice of reaching services through known applications or addresses rather than unverified SMS destinations.|Discard the untrusted destination and verify the claim through an established entry point.|Useful for reducing exposure to account, payment and delivery lures in texts.|A known application or device can still be compromised, and some legitimate services use texts. The practice is not a guarantee.|A customer opens the bank's known app rather than following an alarming text-message link.
112|Mobile endpoint security tools|Device protections that detect or restrict harmful mobile applications, destinations or configurations.|Use supported platform capabilities, management policies and appropriate threat detection.|Useful for managed devices with supported operating systems and a defined response process.|Sandboxing limits what a scanner can observe; social deception and new threats may remain. Tool claims need capability verification.|A managed phone warns about an unapproved risky application and reports its installation to administrators.
113|SMS and text-message filtering|Classification or blocking of suspicious text messages by device, carrier or service policy.|Use sender, content and abuse signals to identify likely unwanted or malicious messages.|Useful as one layer against mass text lures and known malicious patterns.|Spoofed sender information, novel wording and legitimate-looking threads can evade filtering; false positives are possible.|A device moves a known fraudulent delivery text into its spam folder.
114|Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance (DMARC)|Email-domain policy and reporting based on aligned SPF or DKIM results.|A domain publishes policy and receivers assess alignment with the visible From domain under DMARC rules.|Useful against some unauthorized use of a protected domain in email, with carefully deployed enforcement.|It does not certify message content, stop look-alike domains or prevent abuse of a compromised legitimate account.|A company progresses to an enforced DMARC policy after reviewing legitimate mail flows and reports.
115|Financial authorization and fraud controls|Procedures that constrain how payments and account-detail changes are approved.|Use independent verification, separation of duties, transaction limits and reconciled records.|Useful when deceptive communications attempt to turn a valid business workflow into fraud.|Collusion, weak exception paths and compromised approvers can undermine the process. Approval does not replace account security.|A changed payee requires confirmation through a known supplier contact and a second authorized approver.
116|Evidence-based fact checking|Assessing a claim against original evidence and credible independent corroboration.|Check dates, context, source provenance and what the evidence actually supports; retain uncertainty where appropriate.|Useful for suspected misinformation, hoaxes and exaggerated incident claims.|Sources can be wrong or incomplete; popularity and repeated reporting may trace to one unreliable origin.|An analyst finds that an alleged current breach image actually documents a much older unrelated outage.
117|Content moderation procedures|Policy-based review and handling of material on a managed platform.|Apply stated standards through proportionate review, restriction, removal and appeal workflows.|Useful for reducing exposure to harmful or misleading content within a platform's authority.|Moderation is not an automatic truth detector and can be inconsistent or overbroad. Legal and contextual considerations matter.|A forum flags an unsupported security warning for review instead of automatically presenting it as verified guidance.
118|Role-appropriate security instruction|Training tied to the decisions and responsibilities people actually have.|Use realistic tasks, clear verification steps and reinforcement that fits operational workflows.|Useful for impersonation and misleading-information risks alongside enforceable controls.|A course completion record does not prove correct action under pressure or remove a technical weakness.|Reception staff practice a safe response to someone claiming a senior employee's identity.
119|Identity proofing and verification|Establishing that a claimant corresponds to an asserted real-world or organizational identity.|Evaluate appropriate evidence and independent checks using a process proportionate to the risk.|Useful for enrollment, account recovery and issuing replacement credentials when identity must be re-established.|Proofing differs from authenticating a returning user and from authorizing a particular action. Public trivia is weak evidence.|An account-recovery team verifies the employee through approved evidence before issuing a replacement authenticator.
120|Role-based access control (RBAC)|Authorization in which permissions are assigned to roles and identities receive approved role memberships.|Enforce the role's permitted operations at resource boundaries and review membership lifecycle.|Useful for managing consistent job-based access in applications and cluster APIs.|Overbroad roles, inherited rights and compromised members can still cause harm. RBAC does not enforce every workload security setting.|A cluster user can inspect workloads but cannot create privileged administrative resources.
121|Web destination and content filtering|Rules controlling access to web destinations or selected web content.|Block known harmful sites or categories through an assessed proxy, resolver or endpoint enforcement point.|Useful for restricting access to a known compromised site while incident response proceeds.|New destinations, encrypted visibility limits and policy bypasses can reduce coverage. A permitted site can still be malicious.|A known watering-hole site is temporarily blocked for managed browsers during investigation.
122|Endpoint detection and response (EDR)|Collection and analysis of endpoint activity with investigation and response capabilities.|Correlate process, file, identity and network behavior and support actions such as containment.|Useful for investigating activity that signatures alone may miss when telemetry and staff coverage are sufficient.|Blind spots, tampering and alert overload remain possible. An installed agent is not evidence that every attack is prevented.|A browser exploit leads to unusual process behavior, prompting endpoint isolation and forensic review.
123|Security scanning|Inspection of files, systems or configurations for specified threats or weaknesses.|Use an appropriate vulnerability, malware or configuration scanner and verify findings in context.|Useful for identifying known problems before deployment or during investigation.|Scanning scope and signatures limit coverage; unknown behavior and unsafe device classes may be missed. A clean result is not proof of safety.|A removable file is scanned in a controlled environment before an approved transfer.
124|Look-alike domain monitoring|Watching for registrations or changes resembling protected domains and identities.|Compare domain observations with known names and investigate suspicious use before issuing warnings or takedowns.|Useful for early discovery of typosquatting and brand impersonation.|Similarity is not proof of maliciousness, and monitoring does not block access before a response.|An alert identifies a newly registered one-letter variation of an organization's login domain.
125|Browser typo and look-alike warnings|User-facing alerts when a destination resembles a known site or contains suspicious spelling.|A browser or security layer compares destination patterns and prompts the user to verify before proceeding.|Useful as a navigation aid alongside trusted bookmarks and domain verification.|Heuristics can miss deceptive names or warn on legitimate ones; users may ignore prompts.|A browser highlights a near-match domain and offers a route to the previously used genuine site.
126|USB device control|Policy limiting which USB devices or device classes may be used.|Block unapproved storage or other supported classes through operating-system and endpoint controls.|Useful against untrusted removable media when legitimate peripheral needs are assessed.|Storage-only rules may not block a device emulating a keyboard or network adapter. Physical access remains a separate concern.|A managed workstation refuses an unapproved removable storage device found in a hallway.
127|Enforceable security policies|Organizational rules specifying approved behavior, responsibilities and exceptions.|Translate policy into workable procedures and technical enforcement where possible, then monitor adherence.|Useful for removable media, data handling and requests involving trust.|A written rule alone cannot stop a device or authenticate a caller. Unsafe exception practices can defeat the rule.|A media-transfer policy requires approved devices and a controlled inspection route.
128|Access-control vestibules|Physical entry spaces designed to control passage through sequential doors.|Interlocks and authorization checks can limit entry to an assessed number of people at a time.|Useful for restricted facilities with appropriate supervision, safe egress and accessibility design.|Piggybacking detection and emergency behavior depend on the design; unsafe confinement is unacceptable.|A laboratory entry vestibule checks individual authorization before opening the interior door.
129|Electronic badge access systems|Systems that associate a presented credential with access permissions at a physical boundary.|Readers and controllers enforce door rights and retain relevant entry records.|Useful for managed facility access with prompt revocation and secure credential technology.|Shared or cloned badges, tailgating and weak recovery procedures can bypass identity assumptions.|A departed employee's badge rights are revoked across managed office entrances.
130|Physical security personnel|Trained people who monitor facilities, verify access and respond to security events.|Apply approved challenge, escort and response procedures with situational judgment.|Useful where human oversight complements barriers and electronic systems.|Staffing, fatigue, deception and unsafe confrontation risks limit coverage. Guards do not substitute for every technical barrier.|A guard verifies a visitor's sponsor through a known internal contact before entry.
131|Antivirus protection|Detection and remediation of recognized malicious files and related execution behavior.|Use updated signatures and other supported detection techniques with quarantine and investigation.|Useful as an endpoint layer against file-infecting malware and other supported threats.|Novel or concealed malware may evade detection, and removal may not restore trust after privileged compromise.|A file-infecting malware signature triggers quarantine and checks of other executable files.
132|Supported software and device updates|Maintaining components through verified supported releases that correct known defects.|Inventory versions, assess applicability, test where needed and verify deployment of the required update.|Useful when a known security flaw has a supported fix.|An update is not proof that a previously compromised device is clean; unsupported products may require replacement.|A camera fleet receives a tested vendor release addressing a remotely exploitable service flaw.
133|Safer web use|Practices and browser protections that reduce exposure to deceptive or malicious web content.|Use trusted navigation, supported browsers and cautious handling of downloads and unexpected prompts.|Useful for everyday risk reduction in combination with endpoint and identity controls.|A familiar site can be compromised and careful users can still encounter browser exploits.|A user retrieves an application from a verified publisher route rather than an advertisement impersonating it.
134|Network firewalls|Enforcement devices or host controls that permit or block traffic according to configured policy.|Restrict unnecessary services and crossings between network security boundaries.|Useful for reducing reachable paths and limiting propagation through unwanted services.|Allowed flows may carry attacks; policy errors and encrypted visibility limits remain. A firewall does not patch a vulnerable host.|A host firewall blocks an unused service that a worm otherwise could reach.
135|Network segmentation|Dividing a network into security zones with controlled communication between them.|Define allowed paths and enforce them through suitable routing, firewall or equivalent policy.|Useful for limiting lateral movement and reducing blast radius across workloads or device fleets.|An unconstrained VLAN is not sufficient isolation. Permitted vulnerable services and shared management paths can bridge zones.|An IoT camera zone cannot initiate connections to employee workstations.
136|Intrusion prevention systems (IPS)|Controls that inspect activity and can block traffic matching supported attack conditions.|Apply signatures or behavioral rules inline at an assessed enforcement point.|Useful against known exploit patterns with appropriate tuning and sufficient visibility.|False positives can interrupt service, and evasion or encrypted traffic can hide attacks. It does not replace patching.|An IPS rejects a recognized exploit request targeting an unpatched service while remediation is arranged.
137|Application allowlisting|Execution policy permitting only approved applications or code identities.|Rules based on assessed publishers, hashes or other supported attributes limit what can run.|Useful on managed systems with controlled software needs and a maintained approval process.|Approved interpreters and signed vulnerable tools can be abused. Overbroad publisher rules or weak administration reduce protection.|A managed workstation refuses an unapproved fake support utility despite a user's attempt to run it.
138|Behavior-based threat detection|Detection based on suspicious actions and relationships rather than only known file identifiers.|Observe relevant behavior such as unusual persistence, injection or credential access and investigate correlations.|Useful for malware variants and abuse of legitimate tools where sufficient telemetry exists.|Heuristics can be bypassed or mistaken, and detections need operational response.|A signed tool's unusual chain of actions triggers investigation despite its apparently legitimate file signature.
139|Security gateways|Boundary services that mediate and inspect a defined class of traffic.|An email or web gateway can enforce content rules, scan objects and route suspicious activity for review.|Useful when the gateway's specific capability matches a documented delivery path.|The broad label does not imply all-purpose protection. Encrypted visibility, direct paths and unknown threats limit coverage.|A mail gateway quarantines a known malicious attachment associated with a ransomware campaign.
140|Vulnerability patch management|The process of applying and verifying fixes for known software defects.|Prioritize exposure, test operational effects, deploy trusted fixes and confirm vulnerable versions are removed.|Useful when a relevant vendor or maintainer fix exists.|Patching does not retroactively undo theft or persistence and cannot fix a flaw for which no remedy exists.|An exposed service receives a verified security patch after its risk and dependencies are assessed.
141|Service and port exposure control|Restricting which services accept or initiate network connections through firewall policy.|Close unused listening services and apply assessed ingress and egress rules.|Useful for reducing unnecessary access paths to remote-control services.|A Trojan may use an allowed outbound connection or trusted service; port blocking alone is not malware cleanup.|A workstation policy denies unneeded inbound remote-control ports and monitors unexpected outbound sessions.
142|Anomaly detection|Identifying observations that differ from a defined expected pattern.|Compare activity with an assessed baseline or model and send meaningful deviations for investigation.|Useful for unusual destinations, process chains or identity activity when context is available.|An unusual event is not necessarily malicious, and attackers can imitate normal behavior. Baselines require maintenance.|A workstation contacting a new external control endpoint at unusual hours triggers review.
143|Endpoint or workload isolation|Separating a suspected compromised system from sensitive resources while preserving appropriate response access.|Restrict network paths, invalidate sessions where needed and use trusted systems for recovery decisions.|Useful for containing malware and avoiding sensitive work in a compromised browser or endpoint.|Isolation is not eradication; it can disrupt business and must preserve safety and evidence needs.|A suspected infected laptop is isolated from production access while responders collect evidence.
144|Authorized sinkholing|Redirecting selected malicious infrastructure traffic to a controlled defensive destination.|Use appropriate legal and technical authority to alter resolution or routing for a known command endpoint.|Useful for disrupting some botnet control channels and identifying affected clients.|Alternative command paths and embedded addresses can bypass it. Sinkholing does not remove malware and must not capture unrelated traffic.|A coordinated defender operation redirects a botnet domain to a monitored defensive server.
145|Upstream DDoS scrubbing|Filtering attack traffic in sufficiently provisioned infrastructure before forwarding acceptable traffic to the protected service.|Route traffic through a mitigation service that distinguishes assessed attack patterns from legitimate demand.|Useful when local link capacity would otherwise be exhausted before on-premises controls can act.|Application-layer floods, routing setup and false positives require planning; no service has unlimited capacity.|A provider diverts a volumetric flood away from the customer's constrained access circuit.
146|Network traffic analysis|Examining flow, packet and application observations to understand behavior and anomalies.|Measure rates, sources, destinations and protocol behavior and correlate them with service symptoms.|Useful for diagnosing DoS/DDoS and informing appropriate filtering or escalation.|Analysis alone does not absorb or block a flood. Collection visibility and privacy constraints limit conclusions.|Operations staff distinguish a traffic surge from a distributed attack before activating mitigation.
147|Internet service provider coordination|Working with a connectivity provider on upstream visibility and mitigation.|Use established incident contacts to arrange routing, filtering or scrubbing before a constrained customer link.|Useful for attacks whose volume or source cannot be handled at the local edge.|Response capability, lead time and collateral effects vary by agreement and provider. It is not automatic attack prevention.|An organization activates its provider's agreed DDoS escalation procedure during a sustained flood.
148|Transport Layer Security (TLS)|A protocol protecting a network session's confidentiality and integrity with peer authentication as configured.|Negotiate supported cryptography and verify the intended certificate identity and trust chain.|Useful against interception on a protected path when endpoints and trust configuration are reliable.|It does not protect data after a malicious browser decrypts it or prove that a look-alike site's operator is benign.|A client rejects an intermediary's certificate because it is not valid for the intended server identity.
149|Certificate or public-key pinning|Restricting a client to an expected certificate or public-key identity beyond ordinary trust-store acceptance.|A managed application checks an approved pin set during its protected connection process.|Useful in narrowly controlled clients with a robust update, recovery and key-rollover design.|Bad rollover can cause outages; pinning is not generally suitable for arbitrary browser sites and does not cure endpoint compromise.|A controlled device client accepts only the planned service key set and its carefully staged successor.
150|Virtual private network (VPN)|An authenticated encrypted tunnel carrying selected traffic between specified trusted endpoints across another network.|Encapsulate traffic across an untrusted segment after verifying the tunnel peer and enforcing routing policy.|Useful for protecting traffic between the client and trusted tunnel endpoint.|It does not authenticate every visited site, make a hostile Wi-Fi login safe or protect data after tunnel termination.|A remote employee forms a verified organizational tunnel before accessing internal services.
151|Context-aware output encoding|Representing untrusted output so the receiving format treats it as data, not executable syntax.|Choose the encoder for the exact HTML, attribute or other supported output context and use safe rendering APIs.|Useful when displaying plain untrusted text inside a known safe context.|Encoding for one interpreter is not safe for another; some script or URL contexts should not accept interpolation at all.|An application encodes a comment before displaying it as plain HTML text.
152|Content Security Policy (CSP)|A browser-enforced policy restricting resource loading and script execution for a page.|Use carefully designed directives, nonces or hashes and avoid unnecessary unsafe execution allowances.|Useful as defense in depth against some script injection and unexpected resource loading.|It does not replace safe output handling; permissive policy or trusted-script abuse can undermine it.|A site permits only expected scripts with appropriate nonce handling while fixing the underlying injection defect.
153|HTML and content sanitization|Parsing and restricting intentionally permitted rich content to a defined safe subset.|Use a maintained sanitizer suitable for the browser context instead of ad hoc string removal.|Useful when an application deliberately allows limited formatting or markup.|Parser differences, unsafe configuration and later mutation can reintroduce script execution. Sanitization is not needed in place of simple text encoding.|A comment editor retains basic formatting but removes active content through a maintained HTML sanitizer.
154|Path canonicalization and confinement checks|Resolving path representations and verifying that access remains within an authorized resource scope.|Handle encodings, separators and platform rules, then use safe file APIs and a verified allowed root.|Useful when applications accept resource paths rather than opaque identifiers.|Naive string-prefix checks, symbolic links and check/use races can bypass confinement even after normalization.|A download service rejects a resolved file path that escapes its authorized export directory.
155|Chroot filesystem confinement|Changing a process's apparent filesystem root on supported Unix-like systems.|Limit normal path resolution to a designated tree with appropriate permissions and privilege reduction.|Useful as a containment layer in carefully configured services.|Chroot is not a complete sandbox; privileged processes and unsafe open descriptors may escape intended restrictions.|A minimally privileged service receives a restricted filesystem view alongside other isolation controls.
156|Address Space Layout Randomization (ASLR)|Randomizing memory placement to make exploitation that relies on predictable addresses harder.|The platform varies locations of code, libraries, stacks or other mapped regions according to its implementation.|Useful as a memory-exploitation mitigation with adequate entropy and compatible binaries.|Information leaks, limited entropy and code-reuse techniques can weaken it. It does not remove an out-of-bounds memory bug.|A process uses randomized library placement while developers repair a vulnerable parser.
157|Stack protection canaries|Guard values placed around certain stack data to detect selected overwrite patterns.|Compiler-generated checks detect unexpected guard changes before a protected function returns.|Useful as one layer against some stack-based buffer-overflow techniques.|Not every object or overwrite is covered, and a leaked guard may be bypassed. They do not establish memory safety.|A protected program aborts after an oversized input corrupts a stack guard.
158|Restrict or disable NTLM|Reducing or eliminating an assessed Windows NTLM authentication path.|Inventory dependencies, audit usage and enforce appropriate restrictions while migrating required services.|Useful against NTLM-specific credential reuse when supported alternatives and compatibility have been verified.|It can break legacy services and does not stop Kerberos ticket/key abuse or all credential theft. Some paths may remain unless explicitly covered.|An organization retires NTLM for a tested service and verifies that fallback is no longer accepted.
159|Kerberos authentication|A ticket-based network authentication protocol using a trusted key distribution service.|Clients obtain and present service tickets according to domain identity and trust policy.|Useful in appropriately managed environments as part of reducing legacy authentication exposure.|Kerberos is not a universal pass-the-hash remedy; stolen keys or tickets, weak delegation and NTLM fallback remain risks.|A service accepts properly validated Kerberos tickets after its authentication dependencies are reviewed.
160|Hash-based message authentication code (HMAC)|A keyed integrity and authenticity construction based on a cryptographic hash.|Parties sharing a secret compute and verify a MAC over the intended message and context.|Useful when shared-key authentication fits the protocol and keys are protected.|It does not encrypt data, provide public signature verification or automatically supply replay protection. It is not a generic collision repair for every protocol.|A service verifies a request MAC and separately checks the message's freshness.
161|Appropriate modern cryptographic algorithms|Selecting reviewed cryptographic primitives that provide the properties required by a protocol.|Replace deprecated constructions through a tested design with suitable security strength and implementation support.|Useful when old collision-sensitive or otherwise broken primitives remain in use.|An algorithm name alone does not make a protocol secure; modes, trust, keys and implementation details still matter.|A signing workflow migrates from a collision-broken hash to a supported reviewed construction.
162|Virtual local area networks (VLANs)|Logical separation of local network traffic into defined broadcast domains.|Assign interfaces and apply appropriate inter-VLAN routing and access policy at controlled boundaries.|Useful for organizing and separating traffic when enforcement and switching configuration are correct.|A VLAN is not encryption or complete authorization. Misconfiguration and permitted routing can expose traffic across segments.|A guest device VLAN cannot directly reach an administrative segment because routing rules enforce that separation.
163|Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)|A standardized symmetric block cipher used within suitable cryptographic constructions.|A secret key transforms fixed-size blocks; an appropriate mode defines how messages are encrypted and authenticated.|Useful when the algorithm, mode, nonce handling and key lifecycle fit the application.|AES alone is not authenticated encryption, password storage or a complete protocol. Bad mode or key use can defeat the system.|An application uses a reviewed AES-based authenticated mode rather than a proprietary cipher.
164|Cryptographic key management|Controls for generating, protecting, distributing, using, revoking and retiring keys.|Separate access, use suitable randomness and storage, track key versions and plan compromise response.|Useful throughout encrypted and signed system lifecycles, including planned migration and verified compromise response.|Protecting keys does not repair a broken algorithm or unsafe protocol. Losing keys may also make legitimate data unrecoverable.|A service rotates a compromised encryption key and tracks which data requires re-encryption.
165|Reviewed cryptographic standards and designs|Using appropriate established cryptographic constructions instead of unassessed proprietary inventions.|Match required properties to current standards and validated implementation practice, then test the complete protocol.|Useful when selecting or replacing an encryption or authentication design.|Compliance with a label does not prove correct configuration, safe randomness or suitability for every use.|A team rejects a custom substitution cipher and adopts an interoperable reviewed protocol design.
166|Credential and key rotation|Replacing a secret under a defined lifecycle or compromise condition.|Reissue keys or credentials, revoke old use and update dependents through an authenticated process.|Useful after exposure, cryptoperiod limits or algorithm migration where the system design requires it.|Routine password changes without evidence are not universally helpful. Rotation does not fix a weak algorithm or malware stealing the replacement.|A leaked service key is revoked and replaced while affected consumers are updated safely.
167|Certificate authority trust and identity validation|A trust service and validation process binding public keys to asserted identities under a defined policy.|Clients verify certificate chains, intended names, validity and application-specific identity requirements.|Useful for authenticating enterprise Wi-Fi servers and other TLS peers when client trust is configured correctly.|A certificate from any CA is not sufficient for any identity. Misissued certificates, unsafe user overrides and compromised endpoints remain risks.|A Wi-Fi client rejects an authentication-server certificate not valid for its configured organization server.
168|Rogue wireless infrastructure detection|Finding wireless devices or configurations inconsistent with authorized infrastructure.|Combine sensor observations, inventory and network evidence to locate and investigate suspected rogue access points.|Useful for organizational wireless assessment and incident response.|Similar names do not prove malice, and detection does not guarantee prevention before a user connects.|An alert identifies an access point imitating a managed network name and prompts physical investigation.
169|Protected Management Frames (802.11w)|Protection for specified robust Wi-Fi management frames when supported and negotiated.|Authenticate relevant management traffic to reduce forgery such as certain deauthentication messages.|Useful when infrastructure and clients enforce the required protected-management configuration.|Not all management frames are covered and radio jamming remains possible. Compatibility and transition settings need verification.|A modern wireless deployment requires protected management frames for compatible clients.
170|Intrusion detection systems (IDS)|Sensors that identify suspicious activity and raise alerts rather than inherently blocking it.|Inspect network or host telemetry using supported signatures, protocol checks or analytics.|Useful when sensor placement, visibility and incident response make alerts actionable.|Detection gaps and false positives remain; encrypted payloads may be unavailable. An IDS is not automatically an IPS.|A wireless sensor alerts on a burst of suspicious disconnect messages for investigation.
171|Disable unsafe automatic network association|Client policy preventing untrusted or obsolete network profiles from being joined automatically.|Remove unnecessary remembered networks and require appropriately authenticated network configurations.|Useful against deceptive responders exploiting automatic association behavior.|A user can still select a malicious network manually; a remembered name alone never proves network identity.|A managed laptop no longer probes for and automatically joins an old public guest network.
172|Reinforced doors and entry assemblies|Physical barriers designed to resist specified forced-entry conditions.|Strengthen the door, frame, hinges, anchoring and locking assembly together.|Useful for delaying forced access as part of a risk-assessed facility design.|A strong panel with a weak frame can fail. Fire egress, accessibility and alternate routes remain important.|A facility upgrades a vulnerable service entrance with a rated integrated assembly.
173|Physical intrusion alarms|Sensors and notification systems that detect selected access or tamper events.|Door, motion or other detectors trigger monitored alerts with defined response responsibility.|Useful for recognizing and responding to attempted or completed forced entry.|An alarm does not physically stop entry, and false alarms or delayed response can limit benefit.|A forced-open door event reaches a staffed response function with the correct location.
174|Security-Enhanced Linux (SELinux)|A Linux mandatory access-control system enforcing policy beyond ordinary discretionary permissions.|Labels and policy rules constrain which resources and operations a process may use.|Useful for limiting container and host process access when enforcing mode and appropriate policy are maintained.|Permissive mode, overly broad policy and kernel vulnerabilities can undermine confinement. It does not make privileged containers universally safe.|A container process is denied access to host files outside its approved SELinux policy.
175|Kernel resource namespaces|Operating-system mechanisms providing separated views of processes, mounts, networks or other resources.|Assign workloads to appropriately configured namespaces to reduce unintentional sharing and visibility.|Useful as one component of container isolation on a supported patched host.|Namespaces do not alone provide a complete security boundary; capabilities, mounts, host sockets and kernel vulnerabilities matter.|A container has its own process and mount views while separate policy restricts privileged operations.
176|Air-gap and strong network isolation|An architecture separating a system from direct network connections to other environments.|Remove intended electronic network paths and control media transfer, maintenance and human access routes.|Useful where operational and safety needs permit a carefully maintained isolation boundary.|Removable media, laptops, supply-chain compromise and unofficial bridges can cross the gap. Claimed isolation must be verified.|A sensitive control system exchanges approved files only through a controlled transfer procedure.
177|Industrial and SCADA network firewalls|Network boundary enforcement designed for assessed industrial control environments.|Restrict allowed communication and, where supported, inspect relevant industrial protocol operations.|Useful between engineering, supervisory and process zones when rules are developed with operations and safety staff.|An unsafe rule can disrupt physical processes. Firewalls do not replace controller hardening, recovery or independent safety systems.|A plant boundary allows only approved engineering paths and rejects unnecessary controller access.
178|Supplier and system security assessments|Structured evaluation of a supplier, service or system's relevant risks and practices.|Review evidence, dependencies, development practices and response capability, then track specific findings.|Useful for selecting and monitoring supply-chain relationships according to their actual exposure.|A favorable assessment is not a guarantee of future security and may miss hidden compromise. Findings need enforceable follow-up.|A software buyer examines build provenance and incident notification commitments before adopting a dependency.
179|Verified digital signatures|Cryptographic checks tying signed content to a key identity and detecting later modification.|Verify the signature with an independently trusted key and check release context and publisher identity.|Useful for software packages and update authenticity when the signing process is protected.|A valid signature does not prove benign behavior; compromised publishers or signing keys can sign malicious code.|An updater rejects a package altered by a mirror because its signature no longer matches the trusted release.
180|Kubernetes workload admission security policy|Rules that assess workload security settings before resources are accepted into a cluster.|Use supported controls such as Pod Security Admission and appropriate admission policy to restrict privileged workloads and unsafe host access.|Useful for new or changed workloads together with API authorization and runtime protection.|The removed PodSecurityPolicy API should not be deployed as a current feature. Admission policy does not fix every running workload or kernel flaw.|A cluster rejects a manifest requesting prohibited host privileges under its enforced workload standard.
181|Heuristic threat detection|Rules or models that infer suspicious behavior from patterns rather than only exact known signatures.|Evaluate activity such as abnormal process chains or exploit-like behavior and escalate assessed matches.|Useful for finding some new variants or unknown exploits when relevant observations are available.|False positives, evasion and blind spots remain. It does not guarantee detection of a zero-day exploit.|An endpoint flags unusual child-process behavior even though the payload has no known hash signature.
182|Threat intelligence|Assessed information about adversaries, techniques, vulnerabilities or indicators that informs security decisions.|Collect context, evaluate reliability and relevance, then connect intelligence to monitoring and response.|Useful for prioritization, hunting and detection updates when information is timely and applicable.|An indicator can be stale, shared or misleading; intelligence is not automatic blocking and cannot describe every unknown exploit.|A confirmed campaign report helps a team investigate a specific suspicious endpoint pattern.
183|Threat-specific DNS security|A family of controls protecting different parts of name resolution and DNS administration.|Specify whether the objective is DNSSEC validation, protected resolver transport, registrar security or resilient authoritative service.|Useful only when the chosen mechanism is tied to the actual DNS threat path.|Encrypted DNS does not authenticate unsigned zone data, and DNSSEC does not encrypt queries or prevent every registrar-account takeover.|A design separates signed-data validation from encrypted client-to-resolver transport rather than calling both one generic fix.
184|Managed browser extensions|Browser add-ons providing defined capabilities under explicit permission and update controls.|Allow only justified extensions from assessed sources and monitor permissions and changes.|Useful for specific supported browser protections where the extension's authority is appropriate.|Extensions can themselves steal data or alter transactions. Adding an arbitrary extension is not a reliable defense against a compromised browser.|An administrator removes an unapproved extension with broad access to banking pages and investigates the endpoint.
185|Suppress network-name advertisement|A Wi-Fi configuration that omits the SSID from some routine broadcast advertisements.|The network may be absent from a simple visible list, while other management exchanges still reveal identifying information.|At most an administrative presentation choice, not a meaningful substitute for wireless authentication or encryption.|It does not make radio activity or the SSID secret, and probing behavior can create privacy issues. Do not teach it as a wardriving defense.|A wireless survey still identifies a network whose access point suppresses normal SSID advertising.
